<h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
<ul>
<li>Torpor is a short-term, cold-triggered state where a hamster's breathing and heart rate slow dramatically and it becomes cool and nearly motionless &mdash; it is not the same as hibernation and it is not death.</li>
<li>The clearest test for a still, cold hamster is to look closely for any breathing and to check for the stiffness of rigor mortis, which torpid hamsters do not have.</li>
<li>Warming must be gradual: room temperature first, then body heat from cupped hands, never direct heat sources or hot water, which can cause fatal shock in a torpid animal.</li>
<li>Pet hamsters (Syrian and dwarf species) are not natural hibernators the way wild ground squirrels are &mdash; prolonged cold exposure is a welfare emergency, not a normal seasonal behavior.</li>
<li>Keeping the room in a comfortable range and away from drafts, cold floors, and unheated rooms is the actual fix, not letting torpor happen repeatedly.</li>
</ul>

<div class="pz-quick-steps" style="background:#FFF4EC;border-left:4px solid #FF6B1A;border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:16px 20px;margin:24px 0">
<p><strong>My hamster is cold and not moving: quick steps</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Look and listen closely</strong> for breathing (tiny chest movements) before assuming the worst. Torpid hamsters breathe very slowly and can look completely still at first glance.</li>
<li><strong>Do not apply direct heat.</strong> No heating pad against the skin, no hair dryer, no hot water, no microwave.</li>
<li><strong>Warm gradually</strong> using the step-by-step method below, and call an exotic-animal vet as soon as you can, even if your hamster starts to revive.</li>
</ol>
